 :retained
 {:regex "Superseded 2026-09-08. The sentence this entry used to carry --
          \"Host regex is not part of the pure-product profile\" -- was true
          about the HOST regex and was read as though it settled regex
          itself. It did not: root ADR-2608650000 classifies regex as
          :not-yet-implemented, not as a forbidden head. A bounded,
          backtracking-free engine now lives in
          src/kotoba/lang/bounded_regex.kotoba (migration/bounded-regex-v1.edn).
          kotoba.lang.text keeps the host wrappers as the general oracle."
  :split "Collection-returning string split remains deferred in T4.2;
          string-split-count admits and runs on KIR/Wasm but restricted JS
          rejects it as an unsupported KIR operation (measured 2026-08-02)."
  :unicode-construction "Requires bounded codepoint collection design."
  :format "Variadic formatting requires a bounded structural-argument API."
  :trim-and-padding "Tranche 1 covered trim/triml/trimr, blank?, reverse, repeat and both
          pads; tranche 2 (same day) adds replace-first, trim-newline,
          segment-text/count and pad-center. upper-case and capitalize need
          per-character transformation with no construction route (strings
          are immutable; there is no string-from-codepoint) -- that half
          landed as the string-upper LANGUAGE operation on 2026-09-04 (sema
          145e8b5, kir 29163c9, wasm 255c4dd, amu d90665c7). split's
          collection return remains language work."
  :join-text "DEFERRED, measured 2026-09-04: join needs to walk a
          caller-built collection of strings, and this backend admits no
          readable one. typed-list-new lowers on wasm32 but typed-list-nth
          is not qualified there (typed Wasm operation is not qualified);
          the heterogeneous vector refuses :string items (value type is
          outside the safe profile); the legacy pair chain is typed :i64 per
          the frontend's own comment and [:pair :string] is refused the same
          way. segment-text/segment-count-text were landed instead: the two
          single-string/i64 faces of a separator that callers actually reach
          for. join-text is a version bump with its oracle when a readable
          string collection lands."
  :pad-center "Pads by whole FILL strings against byte budgets: the last
          append can overshoot width by up to fill-size - 1 bytes (a fill is
          never split), and the overshoot lands on the RIGHT, where the odd
          single-byte shortfall goes too. Both pinned by golden vectors. The
          CLJC oracle counts code points and cannot overshoot; the divergence
          is the multi-byte-fill case only."
  :index-units "Kernel index-of/last-index-of answer UTF-8 BYTE offsets at
          code point boundaries. clojure.string answers UTF-16 code unit
          indexes. They agree on ASCII and diverge on multi-byte text; both
          layers' docs name the units (the oracle converts via
          utf8-byte-offsets)."
  :whitespace-class "Kernel trim/blank use an ASCII-only whitespace class
          (space, tab, newline, CR, FF, VT). clojure.string's
          Character/isWhitespace answers for Unicode spaces. The class is
          named in both docstrings rather than silently differing."
  :string-substring-refusal "A needle-sized string-substring window can end
          mid-code-point, which the host refuses -- so index-of/last-index-of
          compare code-point-wise (window-eq?) instead of by substring
          equality. Same shape as the pre-existing starts-with-text?/ends-
          with-text?: they answer correctly whenever the prefix/suffix size
          is a text boundary, and refuse only where the refusal IS the
          mismatch; documented here as a kernel-wide property, not fixed
          per-function."}
